# Any .env, anywhere — never commit. Broad on purpose: the e2e suite's
# .env.e2e was the file that prompted this, but the rule that actually protects
# us is the one nobody has to remember to extend when the next tool picks its
# own name (.env.local, .env.test, an app's .env.production).
#
# `.env*` rather than `.env`: the secrets live in the SUFFIXED variants, which
# a bare `.env` entry does not match at all.
.env*
# ...except templates, which exist to be committed and hold no real values.
# Matched by SUFFIX, not by exact name: the file this suite actually tells you
# to copy is `test/e2e/.env.e2e.example`, which an `!.env.example` entry does
# not match — the negation has to be as broad as the rule it is carving out of.
!*.example
!*.sample
!*.template
